summary:
We study the last iterate of standard tabular temporal-difference (TD) learning from a single trajectory of a finite Markov reward process. For discount factor $γ$, write $H=(1-γ)^{-1}$, and let $μ_{\min}$ and $t_{\operatorname{mix}}$ denote the minimum stationary probability and total-variation mixing time. We prove that last-iterate TD achieves sup-norm error at most $\varepsilon$ with high probability using$\widetilde O\left(
  \frac{H^3}{μ_{\min}\varepsilon^2}
  +\frac{t_{\operatorname{mix}}}{μ_{\min}} \right)$ transitions, for $0<\varepsilon\leq1$. This rate holds both for a constant step size selected for the target accuracy and for a decreasing schedule independent of the target accuracy and terminal time. The latter gives a simultaneous guarantee over all times beyond an explicit transient threshold. The statistical term retains the cubic effective-horizon dependence of synchronous TD, and the additive mixing transient has no extra horizon factor. The result allows non-reversible chains, arbitrary initial state distributions, and bounded rewards that may depend on the next state. The proof uses an anchored local Poisson equation in reverse time to control stochastic fluctuations without a mixing-time factor, and a hitting-time compensation identity to bound initialization error. The latter also yields a finer transient in terms of the worst expected reverse hitting time. A bound on the expected cumulative propagation mass extends this argument to decreasing step sizes. A three-state construction with known deterministic rewards gives matching minimax lower bounds for the statistical and mixing terms, up to logarithms, over specified model classes in a slow-mixing parameter regime.

Stochastic optimization models sequential decision-making under uncertainty, typically assuming known input distributions. When these distributions are unknown, \citet{agarwal2026semibandit} give an online-to-offline reduction for monotone stochastic optimization under semi-bandit feedback, where only probed variables are observed. Its expected regret relative to the offline guarantee is $\widetilde O(n\sqrt T)$, where $n$ is the number of variables and $T$ the horizon. For optimal stopping and Pandora's box, \citet{liu2025improved} achieve $\widetilde O(\sqrt{nT})$ using problem-specific algorithms. \emph{Can a general online-to-offline reduction achieve this improved rate?}
  We give such a reduction for strongly monotone stochastic optimization, answering the corresponding open question. Strong monotonicity is defined relative to the chosen offline algorithm. Our oracle-efficient reduction achieves $\widetilde O(\sqrt{nT})$ expected regret while preserving approximation guarantees for oracles satisfying the same condition. It combines Bernstein-type optimistic distribution estimates with a Hellinger stability bound for adaptive policy transcripts, aggregating squared marginal errors weighted by the executed policy's probing probabilities. The rate is optimal up to logarithmic factors for $T\ge n$, even under full feedback.
  Under monotonicity and uniformly bounded coordinate-wise variation, the same algorithm achieves $\widetilde O(n^{3/4}\sqrt T)$ expected approximation regret, improving the previous linear dependence on $n$. The exponent $3/4$ is sharp for its ordinary regret over instances and exact oracle choices. Our framework applies to prophet inequality, Pandora's box, best-of-$K$ selection, stochastic knapsack, stochastic orienteering, and stochastic submodular maximization.

Low reward variance reduces estimation cost, but does not eliminate monitoring of arms that may improve while unobserved. We refine the variance--variation tradeoff and characterize how both costs depend on supplied variance and variation budgets in bounded, non-stationary multi-armed bandits. Let $A$ be the number of arms, $T$ the horizon, $B$ a bound on consecutive mean variation excluding the initial vector, and $Q$ a bound on the sum of the largest per-round reward variances. For every admissible pair $Q,B$, the minimax expected dynamic regret is, within a single logarithmic factor, \[
  \min\{T,\;A+\sqrt{AQ}+(AQBT)^{1/3}
  +\sqrt{AT}\min\{B,\sqrt B\}\}. \] The deterministic boundary $Q=0$ is characterized without logarithms. For small positive budgets, a safe calibration phase identifies a common interval containing all future means of surviving arms. Clipping observations to a wider interval reduces the cost of restarting a prior hybrid static-regret algorithm; an original-mean analytical reference controls its residuals without charging the variation of clipped means. Balancing clipping bias and monitoring closes the small-budget noisy regime. Fixed oblivious constructions give the matching lower bound. We also quantify adaptation costs: on a disclosed deterministic one-improvement family, a stationary-regret budget has a matching worst-case change-regret frontier, including a finite improvement pulse. This implies an $Ω((T/A)^{1/4})$ loss when one policy competes with all supplied-budget deterministic oracle rates. The budget-free low-noise scheduling problem remains separate.
